Article 15
The policies, programs, and activities of PKHP are focused on:
a. implementation of affirmative action, especially in the fields of education, health, law, employment, social affairs, politics, environment, and economy;
b. elimination of gender gaps that result in poverty and impoverishment of women, as well as ensuring respect, protection, and fulfillment of women's basic rights;
c. enhancement of efforts to protect women from various acts of violence, exploitation and discrimination, including prevention and mitigation efforts;
d. development of gender-responsive policies, monitoring of gender-based violence, increase in budget allocation for Women's Empowerment, and for improving women's welfare in each sector and field;
e. development and improvement of regulations and policies of PKHP in various fields of development;
f. implementation of communication, information, and education (KIE) to improve the quality of life of women;
g. formulation of recording and reporting systems, and systems for handling and resolving cases of violence, exploitation and discrimination against women;
h. development of integrated service centers based in hospitals, and community-based centers in Regions, as facilities for protection of women victims of violence, including women victims of domestic violence.

Article 23
(1) To prevent acts of violence against women and children, the Regional Government conducts empowerment and awareness raising to families, parents, and the community by providing information, guidance and/or counseling.
(2) In addition to empowerment and awareness raising as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regional Government undertakes efforts as follows:
a. increasing the quantity and quality of education both formal and non-formal and informal;
b. opening accessibility to obtain education, training, funding, income enhancement and social services;
c. opening employment opportunities for women;
d. building community participation and concern to implement prevention and protection of women and children from acts of violence;
e. building and providing a complete and easily accessible information system;
f. building networks and cooperation with law enforcement apparatus, government apparatus, universities and various non-governmental organizations that work and/or care for women and children; and
g. opening complaint posts for the protection of women and children from acts of violence.
(3) Prevention of acts of violence as referred to in paragraph (1), shall be implemented in an integrated and sustainable manner based on the Regional Action Plan.

Article 9
(1) The PUG Focal Point in each SKPD in the Region consists of officials and/or staff in charge of planning and/or program duties.
(2) The PUG Focal Point as referred to in paragraph (1), has the following tasks: a. promoting Gender Mainstreaming in the work unit;
b. facilitating the preparation of work plans and budgeting of SKPD that are Gender Responsive;
c. implementing training, socialization, advocacy of Gender Mainstreaming to all officials and staff within the SKPD;
d. reporting the implementation of PUG to the leadership of SKPD;
e. encouraging the implementation of Gender Analysis on policies, programs, and activities in the work unit; and
f. facilitating the preparation of gender data in each SKPD.
(3) The PUG Focal Point as referred to in paragraph (1), shall be selected and determined by the head/leadership of SKPD.

Article 22
(1) Regional Governments have the duty to implement efforts to prevent the occurrence of violence against women and children, comprising:
a. managing data and information regarding the protection of women and children victims of acts of violence;
b. conducting socialization of laws and regulations related to the implementation of protection for women and children from acts of violence;
c. conducting education on anti-violence values toward women and children; and
d. conducting monitoring and evaluation of the implementation of protection for women and children from acts of violence.
(2) Regional Governments in carrying out duties as referred to in paragraph (1), have the authority to provide and organize services for victims of violence, among others:
a. providing and facilitating the establishment of integrated services for victims of acts of violence by involving institutions and community elements;
b. encouraging community awareness of the importance of protection for women and children; and
c. conducting community empowerment for the prevention of acts of violence against women and children.

Article 3
The Implementation of Women's Empowerment and Child Protection has the following objectives:
a. to ensure the implementation of policies, programs, and activities for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection in a systemic, comprehensive, continuous, and integrated manner;
b. to increase the commitment of Regional Governments in implementing policies, programs and activities for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection;
c. to increase the effectiveness of the implementation of policies, programs and activities for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection in the Region in a systematic, comprehensive, continuous and integrated manner;
d. to encourage institutions that handle the duties and functions of Women's Empowerment and Child Protection in the Region.

Article 19
(1) The obligations and responsibilities of the Regional Government as referred to in Article 18 letter a, include:
a. implementing policies for the protection of women and children established by the government;
b. establishing policies, programs, and activities for the protection of women and children in the Region;
c. conducting cooperation in the implementation of protection of women and children from acts of violence;
d. providing support for facilities and infrastructure for the implementation of protection of women and children from acts of violence;
e. allocating a budget for the implementation of protection of women and children from acts of violence in accordance with regional financial capacity; and
f. fostering and supervising the implementation of protection of women and children from acts of violence.
(2) In order to carry out the obligations and responsibilities as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regional Government establishes programs and activities for the protection of women and children in the Regional Action Plan as a basis for the Regional Government in implementing protection of women and children from acts of violence.
(3) The Regional Action Plan as referred to in paragraph (2) is stipulated by a Regent Regulation.

Article 8
(1) The PUG Working Group as referred to in Article 7, has the following duties:
a. promoting and facilitating PUG to each SKPD;
b. conducting socialization and advocacy of PUG to Sub-district Heads and Village Heads;
c. preparing work programs every year;
d. encouraging the realization of Gender Responsive Planning and Budgeting;
e. preparing the work plan of the PUG Working Group every year;
f. being responsible to the Regent;
g. formulating policy recommendations to the Regent;
h. preparing the Regional Gender Profile;
i. monitoring the implementation of PUG in each agency;
j. establishing a technical team to conduct analysis of the regional budget;
k. preparing the Regional Action Plan (RAD) for PUG in the Region; and
l. encouraging the implementation of selection and appointment of Focal Points in each SKPD.
(2) The Technical Team as referred to in paragraph (1) letter j shall consist of officials who understand Gender Responsive Budget analysis.
(3) The Regional Action Plan (RAD) for PUG as referred to in paragraph (1) letter k shall contain:
a. PUG in laws and regulations in the Region;
b. PUG in the development cycle in the Region;
c. institutional strengthening of PUG in the Region; and
d. strengthening community participation in the Region.

Article 4
The scope of this Regional Regulation includes:
a. Gender Mainstreaming;
b. Improvement of Women's Quality of Life;
c. Protection of Women and Children;
d. Child-Friendly District/Regency (KLA);
e. guidance and supervision; and
f. financing.

Article 24
(1) The Regional Government is obligated to carry out handling of women and children who become victims of acts of violence, by providing protection services in the form of:
a. complaint services;
b. health services;
c. legal aid;
d. return;
e. rehabilitation, social reintegration, and medicolegal;
f. identification services;
g. psychological services;
h. education and training services; and/or
i. other forms of services.
(2) The provision of protection services as referred to in paragraph (1) is provided for:
a. women who become victims of violence, including victims of trafficking in persons, women in conflict-prone and disaster areas, women workers, elderly women, women with disabilities, women victims of sexual exploitation, women heads of households, and other vulnerable groups of women;
b. children who become victims of acts of violence, exploitation, maltreatment, and neglect.

Article 18
The obligations and responsibilities in the implementation of protection for women and children are the shared responsibility of:
a. Regional Government;
b. community;
c. family; and
d. parents.

